rgbClr val="2FB0DC"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Three Types of Membrane Proteins</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:cxnSp><p:nvCxnSpPr><p:cNvPr id="261" name="Google Shape;261;p36"/><p:cNvCxnSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvCxnSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="457200" y="762000"/><a:ext cx="8229600" cy="0"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="straightConnector1"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln cap="flat" cmpd="thinThick" w="57150"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="2FB0DC"/></a:solidFill><a:prstDash val="solid"/><a:miter lim="800000"/><a:headEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/><a:tailEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:cxnSp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="262" name="Google Shape;262;p36"/><p:cNvSpPr txBox="1"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="4908550" y="995362"/><a:ext cx="4235450" cy="5570537"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr><p:txBody><a:bodyPr anchorCtr="0" anchor="t" bIns="45700" lIns="91425" spcFirstLastPara="1" rIns="91425" wrap="square" tIns="45700"><a:noAutofit/></a:bodyPr><a:lstStyle/><a:p><a:pPr indent="0" lvl="0" marL="0" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="sng"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Peripheral (non-GPI linked) membrane proteins</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t> can be dissociated from the membrane fairly easily during changes in ionic strength like pH changes.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="0" lvl="0" marL="0" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="1200"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="sng"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Amphitrophic and GPI-linked proteins</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t> are linked to the membrane during specific regulatory events and can be reversibly removed.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="0" lvl="0" marL="0" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="1200"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>In the presence of strong detergents, </a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="sng"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>integral membrane proteins</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t> can be removed from the membrane.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="Diagrams of the cell membrane and how it interacts with integral, amphitrophic, and peripheral proteins.&#xA;On the left is an image labeled at the top “integral proteins.” A cell membrane with multicolored hydrophilic heads interacts with multiple proteins. To the right end of the membrane there is a large membrane-spanning protein. It is yellow in the middle, among the tails, and is blue at either end. It is long, with a lobed top with 3 + signs. It is labeled “polytopic” on the left. Above this protein are 2 large blue proteins. One bonds to the lobed top of the integral protein; there is a Ca2+ on the right end (labeled). On the left, an upward curved arrow shows the release of the large blue protein; it appears detached above. Text to the left of the arrow reads, “change in pH; chelating agent; urea; carbonate.” At the bottom of the membrane is a protein labeled “monotopic”; it is smaller and does not span the membrane. Instead, it only reaches about halfway up to the top. The topmost portion, which is in embedded inside the membrane, is yellow, and the bottom portion outside the membrane is blue. Below this diagram is an illustration of a cluster of detergent molecules, shown as pink lipid molecules with heads and tails. From this come two curved arrows. The left arrow indicates the monotopic protein coated on its yellow end with pink lipid molecules; the right arrow indicates the polytopic protein coated in both sides of its yellow region (because it takes up the entire middle portion of the protein). To the right is an image labeled “amphitrophic proteins”. Below is a cross-section of a cell membrane with multicolored heads and the yellow tails oriented toward the middle. Embedded in the top surface is a GPI. It is a pink head with a long red zigzagged tail extending down among the tails. It is connected upward by a black chain to a large blue protein, which is labeled above as a GPI-anchored protein. Phospholipase C, on the right in blue text, points with a red arrow to the GPI and its bonded anchor. Above, it can be seen that phospholipase C has broken the chain holding the GPI-anchored protein, and is now labeled “protein-glycan” on the left. At the bottom, a blue lobed protein is bonded to a neon yellow PIP head. A vertical equilibrium arrow points down to a detached blue lobed protein, with text to the left reading, “biological regulation.&quot;" id="263" name="Google Shape;263;p36"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="17462" y="2133600"/><a:ext cx="4891087" cy="3581400"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>